# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, so without an instruction here it
# converts every file it thinks is text to CRLF on checkout. Most of this repository would
# not care. Two parts of it care a great deal, and both end up **inside a configuration
# that is flashed to a remote**:
#
#   scaffolds/**   the platform state every build starts from. `.preinstall` and
#                  `.postinstall` are shell scripts the remote *executes* during install:
#                  with CRLF the shebang reads `#!/bin/sh\r`, an interpreter that does not
#                  exist, and the remote rejects the configuration. The `platformconfig`
#                  `.dat` files are parsed line by line, so a trailing `\r` corrupts every
#                  value in them.
#
#   linux/*.sh     the USB link helper, run with `sudo` on the user's machine. A bash
#                  script with CRLF fails the same way.
#
# This was not theoretical: a configuration built on Windows on 2026-09-03 was rejected by
# the remote, and the only difference from the working original was `\n` becoming `\r\n` in
# six scaffold files. The builder copies bytes and was blameless - the conversion happened
# at `git checkout`.
#
# `-text` rather than `eol=lf`: these are byte-exact artefacts destined for another device,
# and the correct instruction is that Git must not interpret them as text at all. The
# project's `unpack -> pack` byte-identity rule depends on it.
# Later patterns win, so the catch-all comes first and the exceptions follow it.
*        text=auto

# Data that must never be touched.
*.png    binary
*.bin    binary
*.ezhex  binary
*.gz     binary
*.zip    binary

# Byte-exact artefacts for another device. `-text` rather than `eol=lf` because the
# remote's own configuration *mixes* endings deliberately - `.preinstall`, `.postinstall`
# and every `platformconfig` file are LF, while `.version` and `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF` are
# CRLF - so the instruction is not "use LF", it is "do not interpret these as text".
# A pattern containing a slash is anchored to this file's directory, so
# `scaffolds/**` alone matched nothing - the scaffold lives under `src/afterglow/`.
**/scaffolds/** -text

# Scripts run through a shell, here and on the remote.
*.sh         text eol=lf
